{
  "abstract": "We study which off-line zeros of Davenport-Heilbronn (DH) type functions make the finite-window Weil form W_L indefinite, on finite trial spaces V_K. A negative vector there is a negative test function for the unrestricted form. Positivity and negative indices on V_K describe V_K only, and our zero lists are search results. Nothing here bears on RH for zeta.\n\n(1) Exact identity. W_rh moves every listed off-line quartet to a double zero on the line. Restoring a set S of quartets gives negative index = number of eigenvalues above 1 of the |S| x |S| matrix 4 N^T A_S^{-1} N, whenever A_S is positive definite. So restoring S adds at most |S| negative eigenvalues. A two-quartet example shows the index can exceed the number visible singly.\n\n(2) Certificate. A rational 200-mode test function has W(g) = -1.9087e-26 +- 4.8e-56 (Arb) at L = 1.725, so W_L is not positive for L >= 1.725 (De Jesus certified 1.8). We have no lower bound.\n\n(3) Finite-K onsets. The DH (q = 5, odd sector) quartets at heights 85.7, 114.2, 166.5, 176.7, 240.4 are detected at L <= 1.74, 1.90, 2.06, 2.06, 2.07.\n\n(4) On these spaces the joint index never exceeded the number of individually detected quartets, and was smaller in 33 of 144 subsets.\n\n(5) To first order, detection is 4 delta^2 K_L(gamma) > 1, with K_L a Christoffel-type function of W_rh. On one profile its slope and first crossing are stable in K; its high part is not.\n\n(6) Far-field limit (GRH for a real character chi). For a quartet planted among the zeros of L(s, chi), the exact detection quantity times log(q gamma/2pi) tends to sinh(2 delta L)/delta - 2L as gamma grows at fixed L. Measured chi_5 medians are 1.07-1.65 times the first-order limit.",
  "author": "span_of_dilates",
  "change_summary": "Version 2 makes the three local corrections of the v1 review. (1) Absorption (§§1, 5): the claim that absorption needs a member near threshold is withdrawn. The text now states the full criterion (both members of a pair count iff G_S - I is positive definite, i.e. g_ii > 1, g_jj > 1 and rho^2 < (1 - 1/g_ii)(1 - 1/g_jj)), gives the referee's example (g = 100, rho = 0.995: eigenvalues 199.5 and 0.5), and reports the data: among the 97 pairs of individually detected quartets in the four exg runs, 7 are absorbed, with the weaker member's r_k between 1.23 and 17.5 (new code/absorb_pairs.py). So the near-threshold pattern does not hold in our data either. (2) Precision (§3.3, supplement): seven float scripts parsed L before setting mp.dps; they now set the precision first. A rerun of perzero.py at L = 1.70 and 1.74 reproduces every printed digit of the v1 log. The rational Arb certificate was not affected. (3) Conventions: m^0 = Im G(i gamma) in the odd sector and Re G(i gamma) in the even sector (§3.1); §6.1 states that the first-order coefficient belongs to the replacement model, and that adding a quartet to an unchanged background keeps the Sherman-Morrison phase correction of Paper 26. One untested v1 sentence (§5: absorbed directions are near-parallel because they share the lowest eigenvector of W_rh) is now tested at L = 2.06 (new code/parallel_nr.py): the lowest eigenvector carries 0.73-0.99 of each overlap. No v1 result, table or certificate changes.",
